1. Why XP x64 first
-------------------

XP x64 (and its sibling Server 2003 x64) is the only 64-bit Windows on which
this design is straightforward to develop:

  - No kernel-mode code signing enforcement. Load-time KMCS enforcement began
    with Vista x64. On XP x64 an unsigned legacy/service driver loads without
    test-signing mode, a cross-signing certificate, or boot-menu tricks.

  - No Hyper-V, VBS, HVCI, or Credential Guard. coLinux's model is to take over
    CR3 and the descriptor tables outright. That is unsound under a root
    partition, and co_host_switch_wrapper() already refuses to run when
    CR4.VMXE is set (src/colinux/arch/i386/passage.c). On any modern Windows
    with virtualization-based security on, that bail-out fires permanently.

  - Era-appropriate CPU features. No PCID in use, no SMEP/SMAP, no CET, no
    5-level paging, no KPTI. Each of those is a separate hazard for code that
    switches CR3 and CR4 by hand.

Kernel Patch Protection (PatchGuard) *was* introduced with XP x64, so it is
present. It is probably survivable because the passage code restores the GDT,
IDT, LDT, TR, control registers and MSRs exactly before returning to the host,
so PatchGuard should never observe a modified structure while the host runs.
Treat this as an empirical risk, not a solved problem, and see section 7.

The consequence to accept up front: this port targets a dead-end host. It is
worth doing because it is the cheapest way to get the switch mechanism correct
in 64-bit mode. Forward-porting the *host* side to a signed driver on modern
Windows is a separate project, and under VBS it may not be possible at all.


2. How the 32-bit world switch works
------------------------------------

The switch is a C function call into a hand-written assembly blob that never
returns to its caller. It returns on the *other* operating system's stack.

The blob lives in the "passage page": a 9-page allocation whose first page is
mapped into both address spaces, at the host's own virtual address and at the
guest's fixed CO_VPTR_PASSAGE_PAGE (0xfeaff000). The remaining 8 pages hold
page tables for the temporary address spaces and are only touched by the host.

It is one blob used in both directions. co_current and co_other are
direction-relative macros: in the host driver co_current is host_state, in the
guest kernel it is linuxvm_state.

Sequence (src/colinux/arch/i386/passage.c, macros
PASSAGE_CODE_WRAP_SWITCH / _PRESERVATION_* / _NOWHERE_LAND*):

  a. Push EBX/ESI/EDI/EBP, then read the three arguments at fixed offsets
     16/24/28(%esp). The literal offsets depend on cdecl plus exactly those
     four pushes.

  b. pushfl; cli. Interrupts stay off for the whole switch. There is no IRQL
     manipulation and no CPU pinning on the Windows side; the cli is the only
     safety mechanism.

  c. Save the current side's state into its co_arch_state_stack_t: segment
     selectors, CR0/2/3/4, GDTR/IDTR (6 bytes each), LDTR, TR, ESP/SS, flags,
     return EIP, DR0-3/6/7, and the SYSENTER MSRs.

     Two details worth knowing. TR's descriptor busy bit is cleared in the
     live GDT (andl $0xfffffdff, 4(%edx,%eax,8)) so the eventual ltr does not
     #GP. DR7 is forced to 0x00000700 to disable all breakpoints across the
     transition, and DR0-7 have a shadow copy in the first 0x20 bytes of the
     passage page so that restores can skip unchanged registers.

  d. "Nowhere land": migrate execution from one address space into the other.
     Two variants.

     Non-PAE host: both sides use identical 2-level paging, so paging is never
     disabled. Load a temporary CR3 whose address space maps the passage page
     at *both* virtual addresses at once, then relocate EIP by adding the
     precomputed other_map delta to a return address on a scratch stack inside
     the passage page.

     PAE host: the guest is always non-PAE (linuxvm_state.cr4 = 0), so CR4.PAE
     must be toggled, which requires CR0.PG = 0, which requires running at
     VA == PA. Each temporary address space therefore maps the passage page at
     both its virtual address and its physical address. The code jumps into the
     identity view, rebases every pointer from VA to PA, clears CR0.PG, sets
     CR4 to just the PAE bit, loads the other side's temp CR3, sets CR0.PG,
     rebases everything again, and returns.

  e. Load the other side's real CR4/CR3/CR0/CR2, then lgdt/lidt/lldt, the
     segment registers, and ltr. The real CR3 load is safe because EIP is
     inside the passage page, mapped at the same VA in the target's real
     address space.

  f. Hand over control. Read the other side's saved ESP/SS with a single
     lss (the two fields are deliberately adjacent for this), patch the other
     side's return EIP and flags directly into its stack, switch to that stack,
     then load CS via a *self-modifying* ljmp $0,$0 whose immediate operands
     are written at runtime. popfl, pop the four GPRs, ret.

Interrupts that arrive while the guest runs are not intercepted. The guest's
own IDT is live, so the interrupt enters the guest's entry stub, which forwards
it: the patched common_interrupt calls proxy_interrupt_handler, which sets
operation = CO_OPERATION_FORWARD_INTERRUPT, stores the vector, clears IF in the
*host's* saved flags so the host resumes with interrupts off, and switches. The
host then decodes the vector out of its own saved IDTR by hand, synthesises an
IRET frame with call/pop/pushfl/push %cs/push, and jumps to the real ISR.

FPU and SSE state is never touched by the passage code. It is handled lazily in
guest C code keyed off CR0.TS (which is part of the saved CR0): the guest's #NM
handler fxsaves the host's FPU into a 0x200-byte global, and restores it before
switching back.


3. The shared ABI problem (read this before touching anything)
--------------------------------------------------------------

co_arch_state_stack_t and co_arch_passage_page_t are *not* defined in src/.
They are created by the kernel patch, in

    arch/x86/include/asm/cooperative.h

and pulled into the host driver via <linux/cooperative.h> from
src/colinux/common/common.h. Both halves must change in lockstep.

Every field offset used by the assembly is a preprocessor string constant
sitting next to the field it describes. It has to be: the passage code is a
file-scope asm() blob assembled by string concatenation, and top-level asm
accepts no C operands, so there is no way to pass offsetof() in.

Upstream wrote those strings by hand with nothing checking them against the
struct, so widening a field silently desynchronised the assembly from the C
declaration. As of patch/abi-offsets-asserted.diff each offset has one numeric
definition, stringified for the assembly, and an assertion against
__builtin_offsetof, so that mismatch is now a compile error:

    unsigned long cr3;
    #define CO_ARCH_STATE_STACK_CR3_N  0x10
    #define CO_ARCH_STATE_STACK_CR3    CO_STRINGIFY(CO_ARCH_STATE_STACK_CR3_N)
    ...
    CO_ASSERT_STATE_OFFSET(cr3, CO_ARCH_STATE_STACK_CR3_N);

All 31 are covered. This is what makes the widening described below safe: any
offset not updated alongside its field fails the build instead of corrupting a
world switch.

Two independent hazards compound this:

  - Every field is `unsigned long`. Win64 is LLP64, so `unsigned long` stays
    4 bytes in the host driver; Linux x86-64 is LP64, so it becomes 8 bytes in
    the guest kernel. A Win64 driver and an LP64 guest would disagree about the
    layout of the page they share, including params[].

  - The code buffer is code[0x260] (608 bytes), size-checked at runtime in
    co_monitor_arch_passage_page_init(). REX prefixes, 64-bit immediates and
    the extra MSR swaps will overflow it, and growing it moves the state
    blocks, which is itself another ABI change.

First concrete task of the port, before any assembly is written: replace the
hex strings with generated constants. Emit them from a small host-side program
that uses offsetof() on the real struct (the kernel's own asm-offsets.c is the
model), make both the driver and the guest include the generated header, and
switch every field to an explicit fixed-width type (uint64_t / co_u64_t). Do
this while the tree is still 32-bit and still builds, so the change is
verifiable against a working baseline.


4. What has to change
---------------------

4.1 Paging and the nowhere-land trampoline

The PAE variant's core trick is now illegal. Clearing CR0.PG while EFER.LMA is
set faults; long mode requires PG=1 and PAE=1. Leaving long mode to toggle
paging means clearing EFER.LME from a 1:1-mapped 32-bit code segment, which is
a large amount of new machinery.

The good news is that it is unnecessary. If host and guest are both long mode
with 4-level paging, there is no paging-mode change to make, and a plain
mov to CR3 between two PML4 trees suffices. The PAE variant collapses into
something shaped like the current SHORT variant, and the PAE/non-PAE axis of
blob selection disappears entirely — four blobs become two, split on the
SYSCALL-MSR axis instead of SEP.

Better still: pick a passage-page virtual address that is free in *both* the
Windows x64 kernel half and the Linux x86-64 kernel half. The 64-bit address
space is vast, so this is easy, and if the two VAs are identical then other_map
is zero in both directions and the entire EIP-relocation dance disappears. This
is the single largest simplification available in the port; design for it.

Remaining work here:

  - Add CO_ARCH_PML4_SHIFT (39) and friends to mmu.h; replace the 2-level and
    3-level temporary address-space builders (short_/normal_/pae_temp_address_
    space_init) with one 4-level builder.
  - Use RIP-relative addressing instead of the call/pop EIP tricks.
  - Widen the page-base masks: andl $0xFFFFF000 truncates any address above
    4 GB and must become a 64-bit, sign-extension-aware mask.
  - Widen physical addresses: co_pfn_t, linux_pte_t/pmd_t/pgd_t, and
    co_os_virt_to_phys() (which currently truncates MmGetPhysicalAddress()'s
    QuadPart to unsigned long). Drop the manager's refusal to run on hosts
    with more than 4 GB, and fix co_monitor_getpp()'s hardcoded 4-byte stride.
  - PTE construction must carry NX and the high physical bits.

4.2 Long-mode CPU state that is currently not saved at all

None of the following is in the state block today, and all of it must be:

  - EFER (0xC0000080) — LME/LMA/NXE/SCE are per-context.
  - The SYSCALL MSRs: STAR (0xC0000081), LSTAR (0xC0000082),
    CSTAR (0xC0000083), FMASK (0xC0000084).
  - The segment-base MSRs: FS_BASE (0xC0000100), GS_BASE (0xC0000101),
    KERNEL_GS_BASE (0xC0000102).

The segment-base MSRs are the dangerous one. In long mode, writing a selector
to %fs or %gs does not restore the 64-bit base. So the existing code — which
saves and restores only the selectors, and deliberately zeroes FS and GS
mid-switch "to be on the safe side" — would destroy the host's per-CPU pointer.
Windows x64 keeps the KPCR at GS base; Linux x86-64 keeps per-CPU data there.
Swapping these via MSRs (or swapgs) is mandatory, and the ordering relative to
the GDT reload matters.

Also note the current SYSENTER handling truncates: rdmsr keeps only EAX, and
the restore path does xor %edx,%edx before wrmsr. On a 64-bit host that
destroys the upper half of SYSENTER_EIP/ESP. Every MSR save/restore must become
a full 64-bit EDX:EAX pair.

FPU/SSE needs rethinking rather than porting. The lazy CR0.TS scheme depends on
#NM and clts, which modern kernels have moved away from (eager FPU), and a
0x200-byte FXSAVE area cannot hold AVX state. Move to explicit XSAVE/XRSTOR
with an XCR0-derived, 64-byte-aligned area saved on *both* sides, and add XCR0
to the state block. This is not optional on x64: the Windows x64 ABI makes
XMM6-XMM15 callee-saved, and compiler-generated code uses SSE pervasively.

Consider also PAT, CR8/TPR, and on modern hosts SPEC_CTRL. For XP x64 these are
mostly moot.

4.3 Descriptors, TSS, interrupt delivery

  - GDTR/IDTR grow from 6 to 10 bytes, which reshuffles every offset in the
    state block. See section 3.
  - The TSS descriptor is 16 bytes in long mode and spans two GDT slots, so the
    busy-bit clear (scale-8 indexing, bit 9 of the second dword) needs a new
    stride and bit position, on both the host and guest sides. The long-mode
    TSS holds RSP0-2 and IST1-7 and has no per-task I/O permission semantics,
    so the guest's co_early_cpu_init needs rewriting.
  - IDT gates are 16 bytes: the guest's idt.size becomes 256*16 - 1. More
    importantly, the host's interrupt re-injection decodes an 8-byte gate by
    hand and must instead reassemble a 64-bit offset from three fields and
    honour the IST index.
  - ljmp with immediate operands does not exist in 64-bit mode, so the
    self-modifying far jump must go. Replace it with push CS / push RIP /
    lretq, or an indirect far jump through m16:64 — or drop it entirely if both
    contexts share a flat 64-bit CS, since in long mode CS is essentially just
    a mode and CPL selector. This also removes the requirement that the passage
    page be writable-and-executable.
  - lss survives in 64-bit mode (unlike lds/les) and can take m16:64 with
    REX.W, but SS has no meaningful base or limit in long mode, so there is no
    longer a reason to keep the SS and RSP fields adjacent. Prefer a plain
    mov to RSP and drop the coupling from the struct layout.
  - iretq always pops five quadwords, even for a same-CPL return, so the
    synthesised interrupt frame becomes 40 bytes with a valid RSP/SS and 16-byte
    alignment. The current frame pushes only flags/cs/eip.
  - Guest entry state: CR4 must gain PAE (and PGE), EFER must have LME/LMA and
    NXE set, and the initial stack must not hardcode 0x2000 — take THREAD_SIZE
    from the guest's own headers, since it differs between architectures and
    between kernel versions.

4.4 Calling convention and register set

The blob reads its arguments off the stack at literal offsets. On x86-64 they
arrive in registers — and the two directions use *different* conventions, since
the host driver is Win64 (RCX/RDX/R8) and the guest kernel is SysV
(RDI/RSI/RDX). Either normalise with a small per-side thunk, or change the
interface so the blob takes a single pointer argument.

R8-R15 must be saved; they do not happen to be preserved the way EBX/ESI/EDI/
EBP were. The Win64 callee-saved set is RBX, RBP, RDI, RSI, R12-R15 and
XMM6-XMM15, which is considerably larger than what the prologue pushes today.

4.5 Pseudo-physical memory and the guest address-space window

The guest's fixed window is CO_VPTR_BASE = 0xffc00000 and about 20 MB below it,
all 32-bit constants. It must move into the canonical 64-bit kernel half, and
the self-map — where one PTE page happens to cover the whole 4 MB special
region, letting the guest rewrite its own top-level PTEs — has to be re-derived
for 4-level paging, where a PT page covers 2 MB.

The pseudo-physical RAM design needs rebuilding, not adjusting. Today a flat
array of 4-byte PTEs indexed by guest PFN simultaneously serves as the page
tables for the linear map of pseudo-physical RAM at CO_ARCH_KERNEL_OFFSET
(0xc0000000). For x86-64 that means 8-byte PTEs, 2 MB PD granularity, 4-level
PGD population, and PAGE_OFFSET at 0xffff880000000000 (2.6.x) or
0xffff888000000000 (modern kernels). Relatedly, pp_pfns is a fixed 1024x1024
two-level shadow that exactly covers 4 GB and needs to become a real multi-level
or radix structure.

CO_LOWMEMORY_MAX_MB (984) is a *derived* number — it is how much pseudo-RAM PTE
array fits in the window — so it changes with the new layout rather than being
tuned.

4.6 Things to delete rather than port

  - antinx.c. RESOLVED, and it has no successor on x86-64.

    It hand-walks the host's live page tables to clear NX on the passage page,
    because MmAllocateNonCachedMemory returns non-executable memory. Measured on
    XP x64 with CO_MANAGER_IOCTL_PROBE_PASSAGE, allocating a real 15-page passage
    page through each allocator and reading back its own PTE:

        MmAllocateNonCachedMemory     0x80000000b024b97b   NX set    uncached
        ExAllocatePool(NonPagedPool)  0x00000000089ef963   NX clear  cached
        MmAllocateContiguousMemory    0x000000000c058963   NX clear  cached

    So the passage page now comes from co_os_alloc_exec_pages(), which uses
    NonPagedPool -- NonPagedPoolNx did not exist until Windows 8. Nothing walks
    or edits a host page table, which is what PatchGuard would object to.

    NonPagedPool rather than contiguous memory because physical contiguity is not
    required: the passage code takes virt_to_phys of each sub-structure
    separately. Allocations of a page or more are page-aligned, which the
    page-table sub-structures do need.

    Note what else that table shows: the existing allocator is not merely
    non-executable but *uncached*. The i386 port has been executing its world
    switch out of uncached memory since 2004. Switching it to the same allocator
    would remove antinx there too and make the switch considerably faster, but
    that is an untestable change here -- there is no 32-bit host to try it on --
    so it is left alone deliberately.
  - co_i386_has_cpuid(). It probes EFLAGS.ID with pushfl/popl, which is
    meaningless on x86-64 (CPUID is architectural) and will not assemble.
    The capability query itself needs leaf 0x80000001 for NX/LM and leaves
    0x7 and 0xD for XSAVE.
  - The FS/GS-nullify step and the SS reload become no-ops or hazards in long
    mode; the real work moves to the base MSRs (4.2).
  - apic.c is a stub and its feature check is wrong anyway (it tests
    caps[0] & CO_ARCH_X86_FEATURE_APIC, i.e. & 9, rather than & (1 << 9);
    compare the correct 1 << CO_ARCH_X86_FEATURE_SEP test in passage.c). Fix
    or remove it during the port.


5. Milestones
-------------

M0. Build the 32-bit tree on a current host.  DONE for the Windows side.
    comake is ported to Python 3, and the WinNT driver and daemons build clean
    on mingw-w64 16.1 / GCC 16 against patched 2.6.33.7 headers. See
    doc/building-modern for how to reproduce it and what is deliberately left
    out: only the FLTK/wx consoles and the WinPcap daemon, each of which needs
    a third-party library built by hand.

    Not done: a bootable 32-bit reference. That needs a built vmlinux, which
    needs a period-appropriate GCC, and then runs into the daemon's
    __GXX_ABI_VERSION check refusing a guest built by a different compiler than
    the daemons (doc/building-modern section 6). The intended way out is M1,
    which makes that check unnecessary, rather than reconstructing a 2011
    toolchain.

    Note the 32-bit build is not runnable on the XP x64 target at all: a
    kernel-mode driver must match the kernel's architecture, so XP x64 will not
    load a 32-bit linux.sys. WOW64 would run the 32-bit daemons, but the driver
    is the part that matters. The first artifact testable on that hardware is
    the 64-bit driver of M2.

    A behavioural reference therefore means a separate 32-bit environment. The
    decision taken is not to install 32-bit XP on the M92p, and instead to stand
    up a VM with the official prebuilt 0.7.9 binaries if and when something looks
    suspicious. Worth validating that coLinux runs stably in that VM before
    relying on it -- coLinux seizes CR3 and swaps the descriptor tables, its own
    CR4.VMXE guard does not fire inside a guest, and an unvalidated reference is
    worse than none because its quirks get blamed on the port.

M1. Make the ABI mechanical, still 32-bit.  DONE.
    All 31 CO_ARCH_STATE_STACK_* / CO_ARCH_STATE_SYSENTER_* offsets now have a
    single numeric definition, stringified for the assembly, and an assertion
    against __builtin_offsetof. patch/abi-offsets-asserted.diff.

    Note the offsets cannot simply be replaced by offsetof(): the passage code
    is a file-scope asm() blob built by string concatenation and top-level asm
    takes no C operands, so they have to remain preprocessor strings. Asserting
    them is the available win, and it is the important one -- a field widened
    without its offset updated is now a compile error instead of the switch
    silently reading the wrong words of the other side's saved state.

    Deliberately not done here: widening the 44 unsigned long fields to explicit
    fixed-width types. On i386 that is a no-op, so it buys nothing on its own,
    and it drags in knock-on work (the %08lx formats in
    co_passage_page_dump_state, narrowing at the assignment sites). It belongs
    with the x86-64 struct definition in M2, where the assertions added here are
    what make it safe -- every offset missed becomes a build failure.

M2. 64-bit host scaffolding that compiles and loads.  DONE, VERIFIED ON HARDWARE.
    COLINUX_ARCH=x86_64 produces a PE32+ x86-64 native-subsystem linux.sys with
    minimum OS 5.02, entry at DriverEntry, importing ntoskrnl.exe, HAL.dll and
    NDIS.SYS, plus seven 64-bit daemons. arch/x86_64 is written from scratch;
    ref/CoLinux64's directory of that name is i386 copy-paste and was useful only
    as a list of files needing attention.

    Loaded and initialised on the M92p under XP x64. colinux-daemon
    --status-driver reports:

        current state: 4 (fully initialized)
        current number of monitors: 0
        current linux api version: 14
        current periphery api version: 22

    State 4 is CO_MANAGER_STATE_INITIALIZED, the top of the enum, so debug init,
    arch init and OS-dependent init all succeeded -- arch init being the new
    x86_64 CPUID query, long-mode check and APIC check. Note the periphery
    version is confirmed by the output existing at all: co_manager_status()
    returns VERSION_MISMATCHED and the caller bails before printing anything if
    it is not 22.

    So the assumptions the XP x64 target rests on are now facts: mingw-w64
    produces a loadable x64 kernel driver, XP x64 loads it unsigned with no
    test-signing, the PE32+ layout and undecorated DriverEntry are right, the
    ioctl path works both directions, and PatchGuard does not object to the
    driver being resident.

    Two bugs were found by doing this, both in the previous commit: base
    relocations applied twice (the build linked with --base-file plus dlltool
    --output-exp, duplicating what modern ld already emits), and the driver
    installing SERVICE_AUTO_START, which turned a bugcheck into a boot loop.
    The relocation bug affected the i386 driver equally and had never been found
    because nobody had run it.

    Lesson worth keeping: simulate the loader applying every relocation before
    shipping a driver. It is cheap, needs no hardware, and would have caught it.

M3. The switch, tested against a trivial guest.
    Write the x86-64 passage code, and bring it up against a hand-written page
    of 64-bit code that does nothing but write a sentinel value into the
    passage page and switch back. Do not involve Linux.
    This is the crux of the project and the milestone most likely to consume
    the most time. Decoupling it from a Linux guest is what makes it debuggable
    at all: a bugcheck here means the switch is wrong, not that a kernel failed
    to boot.
    Exit criterion: host to dummy-guest round trip on XP x64, repeatedly,
    without bugchecking, with all host state verifiably intact.

M4. A 64-bit Linux guest.
    Port the cooperative patch's arch half: the address-space window, 4-level
    page table handling, entry/exit stubs, interrupt forwarding, early printk
    through the passage page. Then serial console, then a cobd root filesystem.
    Exit criterion: 64-bit Linux boots to a shell under XP x64.

M5. Devices: conet, cofs, scsi, the consoles.

M6. Forward-port the guest to a current kernel.

On M4 there is a real choice: port 2.6.33 to x86-64 first, or go straight to a
modern kernel. OPEN, deliberately deferred until the switch works.

The case for 2.6.33 first was that its cooperative patch is a known-good
reference for everything that is not architecture-specific. Measuring the patch
weakens that considerably: 22,809 added lines across 31 patches, the base patch
alone touching 67 files, and seven mentions of x86_64 in the whole thing. What it
patches is entry_32.S, head_32.S, init_32.c, pgtable-2level.h, pgtable_32.h,
io_32.h -- the interrupt entry stubs, early boot, and two-level paging, which
does not exist in long mode. Nearly all of the work is exactly the part with no
x86-64 counterpart, so the reference buys less than claimed, and taking that road
means doing the hard half twice.

Against that, a modern kernel brings KASLR, KPTI, eager FPU and a rewritten early
boot. But it is the target actually wanted, it builds with the toolchain already
in use, and it sidesteps the __GXX_ABI_VERSION mismatch entirely.

Either road needs the world switch working first, so this does not block M3.

Superseded recommendation, kept because the reasoning is still worth weighing:
2.6.33 first.


6. Toolchain
------------

See doc/building-modern for the working recipe and the exact toolchain versions.
Summary of what matters for the port:

  Python 3.14        comake runs on it
  GCC 16.1.1         builds the host side; far too new for a 2.6.33 kernel
  mingw-w64 16.1     i686-w64-mingw32- and x86_64-w64-mingw32- both present,
                     so the 64-bit host compiler is already available
  wine               not installed; only needed for `make installer`

The cross prefix is now derived from COLINUX_ARCH, so building for x86-64 is
COLINUX_ARCH=x86_64 once src/colinux/arch/x86_64 exists. WINVER is already set
to 0x0502 for the x86_64 target and left at 0x0500 for i386.

The guest kernel still needs a period-appropriate compiler, and the daemon's
__GXX_ABI_VERSION check currently forbids mixing it with a modern host
toolchain. M1 is what removes that constraint.


7. Open questions and risks
---------------------------

  - PatchGuard on XP x64. Believed survivable because all state is restored
    before the host resumes, but unproven. Mitigation if it bites: keep the
    host's own GDT/IDT untouched (only ever load the guest's while the guest
    runs), and avoid antinx-style page-table edits entirely (4.6).

  - Test environment. XP x64 on real hardware or in a VM? Nested — running a
    design that takes over CR3 and CR0 inside another hypervisor — is where
    the CR4.VMXE bail-out was pointed in the first place. Expect to need real
    hardware, or at minimum expect VM behaviour not to be representative. This
    needs deciding before M3, because M3 cannot be validated without it.

  - Debuggability. There is a doc/debugging for the 32-bit case. Plan the
    equivalent for x64 before M3: WinDbg over serial or 1394 against XP x64,
    plus the sentinel-in-passage-page technique, because printf debugging
    across a half-completed world switch does not work.

  - Whether the guest should be long mode at all. A compatibility-mode
    (32-bit) guest under a 64-bit host would avoid the entire guest-kernel
    port, keeping the existing i386 cooperative patch. It does not avoid the
    switch work: the host side still needs long-mode state handling, and it
    reintroduces the leave-long-mode problem that section 4.1 says to design
    away. It is probably a false economy, but it is cheap to evaluate and worth
    ten minutes of thought before committing to M4.

  - This design does not generalise forward. See section 1. If the real goal is
    modern Linux on modern Windows, the answer is a VT-x/AMD-V root-mode host,
    not a passage page. The XP x64 target is worth pursuing on its own terms;
    it is not a stepping stone to a modern host.
